-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Sparse View Instability:** Decreasing input views causes significant geometry degradation in standard 3DGS because scene geometry relies on Gaussian primitive positioning, which becomes incorrectly learned under sparse constraints [S1]. DNGaussian mitigates this with depth regularization [S1].
-*   **Overfitting:** Standard spherical harmonics can overfit on sparse views. DNGaussian uses a Neural Color Renderer to relieve this [S1].
-*   **Hollowness:** Without Soft Depth Regularization, surfaces may become semitransparent and cause hollowness [S1].
-*   **Invisible Areas:** 3DGS-based methods expose black background on empty spaces, causing lower metrics in PSNR/SSIM for invisible areas compared to NeRF-based methods that interpolate colors [S1].
+## Subtopics
+
+### Depth Regularization
+The parent method family. DNGaussian builds on [Depth Regularization](7616-depth-regularization.md) by applying it to 3D Gaussian primitives with specific freezing strategies.
+
+### 3D Gaussian Splatting Training
+The base optimization framework. DNGaussian inherits the gradient descent and primitive management from [3D Gaussian Splatting Training](52-3d-gaussian-splatting-training.md) but replaces spherical harmonics with a Neural Color Renderer.
+
+### Radiance-field Optimization
+The broader category of optimizing radiance fields. DNGaussian is a sparse-view variant.
+
+### Differentiable and inverse volume rendering
+The underlying rendering technique. DNGaussian uses differentiable volume rendering for depth and color.
